{
  "id": "Compound::DB00401 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Nisoldipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is linked to Hypertension (disease) through its inclusion in the dihydropyridine class, which includes isradipine and nifedipine. The shared ancestry of Nisoldipine with these compounds, through the common ancestor Methyl Ester and Calcium Channel Blocker, suggests a shared pharmacological mechanism. This class of drugs is known to inhibit vascular smooth muscle contraction, thereby reducing blood pressure. The evidence from multiple paths indicates that Nisoldipine, as a calcium channel blocker, exerts its therapeutic effect by modulating vascular tone, a mechanism that is well-established in the treatment of hypertension. The recurring emphasis on calcium channel blockade and its role in blood pressure regulation across different pathways supports the conclusion that Nisoldipine is a valid candidate for repurposing in the management of hypertension.",
